<Base lang="en" title="Assessment — TailorMind">
  <h1>Assessment overview</h1>
  <p>One assessment, two observation points. We score the same three dimensions — <strong>Criterio</strong>, <strong>Autonomy</strong>, <strong>Tech</strong> — first from your take-home repo, then again live in the technical interview. Same rubric, two signals.</p>

  <h2>The three scores</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Criterio</strong> — judgment under uncertainty: trade-offs you can name, how you separate decision quality from outcome quality, what you cut and why.</li>
    <li><strong>Autonomy</strong> — driving ambiguous work to a shipped result without micromanagement. Documented assumptions count more than asked questions.</li>
    <li><strong>Tech</strong> — full-stack engineering quality: code, structure, tests where they matter, deploys that work.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>1. Take-home <small>(required)</small> — 30 minutes, and it does not fit</h2>
  <p><strong>Required for everyone:</strong> without a submission we don't score and don't move to the interview. The full task lives at <a href={`${b}/en/take-home/`}>/en/take-home</a> — read it before applying if you want. Target time <strong>under 1 hour</strong>, no hard cap. We record the elapsed time between application submission and the moment you paste your repo URL into the form (optimizing the LLM workflow matters).</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Stack is fixed: LÖVE (love2d) + Lua. Nothing else to install.</li>
    <li>The task is not published: it is drawn per candidate and downloaded when the run opens, so cloning today is worth what cloning last week would have been.</li>
    <li>Deliverable: a public GitHub repo + live URL.</li>
    <li>Submission: reopen your application form using the edit link from your confirmation email, paste the repo URL into the "Take-home repo URL" field.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>2. Technical interview — the same three scores, live</h2>
  <p>Same Criterio / Autonomy / Tech rubric, applied to the conversation. We talk about your delivery: why you cut what you cut, what you asked, what you assumed. 40 minutes, short questions and short answers.</p>
  <p>You also bring a <strong>decision journal</strong> with 3 recent decisions, written beforehand:</p>
  <ol>
    <li>What you decided.</li>
    <li>Why — including the alternative you rejected.</li>
    <li>What you expected to happen.</li>
    <li>Your confidence at the time (%).</li>
  </ol>
  <p>We walk through them looking for resulting — judging a decision by its outcome — and hindsight bias. No trick questions.</p>

  <h2>3. Final interview</h2>
  <p>With the other founder. Fit, terms, and the uncomfortable questions you should be asking us: how much runway, where revenue comes from today, what happens if it does not work. We answer with numbers.</p>

  <h2>What good looks like</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>You separate the quality of your decision from the quality of the outcome.</li>
    <li>You update beliefs in small increments when evidence shifts — not at all, or wholesale.</li>
    <li>You can name the cost you traded off (more research vs faster decision vs opportunity).</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
